Hermit kernel (hermit-rs tag hermit-0.13.0, kernel ef27b79): drive a GICv2 as
well as a GICv3, on aarch64 (Chitala H0.1).

Upstream, the kernel drives only a GICv3 (arm_gic::gicv3), and panics on any
other interrupt controller. The ZynqMP (ZCU102, Kria K26, Ultra96-V2), the
BCM2712 (Raspberry Pi 5) and the Tegra X2 all have a GICv2, so the Chitala
Native image could not run as a guest on any of them.

With this patch the kernel reads the controller's kind from the device tree
(/intc: arm,gic-v3 or arm,gic-v4; arm,gic-400, arm,cortex-a15-gic,
arm,cortex-a9-gic or arm,cortex-a7-gic). A GICv3 is driven as before: its
CPU interface is system registers, reached without a lock. A GICv2 is driven
by a minimal driver (src/arch/aarch64/kernel/gic.rs) that does what Linux's
does, which runs both on a GICv2 without the Security Extensions (QEMU virt,
gic-version=2) and on a hypervisor's virtual CPU interface (libvmm on seL4):
interrupt groups are left as they are, an interrupt is acknowledged through
GICC_IAR and ended through GICC_EOIR with the whole acknowledged value, and a
shared peripheral interrupt is routed to the CPU that enables it.
arm-gic's own GICv2 driver puts every interrupt in Group 1 and uses the
aliased registers, which neither of those delivers. The controller's lock
now masks interrupts while held, so an interrupt never waits for it on the
same CPU.

native/run.sh applies it after Chitala's other kernel patches. It is local
platform enablement, not proposed upstream yet (docs/native/carried-patches.md).

